.TH TERMPROBE 1 "October 2026" "termprobe 1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
termprobe \- find out what survives the trip between a program and its terminal

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B termprobe
[\-\-timeout SECONDS] [\-\-label TEXT] [\-\-raw] [\-\-input | \-\-tvision] [\-\-dos [\-\-no\-visual] [\-\-no\-mouse]]
[\-\-no\-vt\-input]

.SH DESCRIPTION
.B termprobe
asks the controlling terminal, in one burst, the questions a capability\-negotiating full\-screen program
(tvision, notcurses, vim, ...) asks, and then checks what came back \- not just whether something did.
Run it in every terminal, shell and ssh combination you care about and compare the answers: the terminal
emulator, ConPTY, Cygwin's console layer, ssh and a multiplexer can each answer, rewrite, delay, split or
swallow what passes through them.

It talks to the controlling terminal (\fI/dev/tty\fP on POSIX systems: Linux, WSL, macOS, Cygwin, MSYS,
mintty; \fICONIN$\fP and \fICONOUT$\fP with native Windows Python), so it works with standard input and output
redirected. Run it on the machine where the program under test runs, after the ssh hop, not before it.

.SS Queries
.TP
OSC 10, OSC 11
Foreground and background colour.
.TP
DA1, DA2, XTVERSION
Who the terminal says it is.
.TP
CSI 18t, 14t, 16t
Size in characters, size in pixels, size of one cell.
.TP
CSI 6n
Cursor position.
.TP
kitty keyboard, modifyOtherKeys
Which key encodings the terminal supports.
.TP
DECRQM 1000, 1006, 2004, 2026, 9001
Mouse, SGR mouse, bracketed paste, synchronized output, win32\-input\-mode.

.PP
A terminal that does not know a query stays quiet; that is listed as "no reply", not as a fault. The whole
timeout is waited unless every query has been answered: ConPTY answers DA1 itself, at once, while the other
queries have to travel to the terminal and back, so DA1 arriving first proves nothing.

.SS Verdicts
Two verdicts are printed.
.TP
.B OSC 11
\fBANSWERED\fP: the colour reply came back.
\fBDROPPED\fP: something answered, but not the colour query (ignored, or eaten on the way).
\fBSILENT\fP: nothing came back at all.
.TP
.B NEGOTIATION
\fBCLEAN\fP: every reply that came is whole, single and consistent.
\fBMANGLED\fP: a query was answered twice (two layers both replied), bytes came back that are no known reply
(garbage that would land on a program's screen), the size disagrees with the real window, or DA1 is conhost's
canned \(lq1;0\(rq.

Things worth knowing that are not faults are printed as "note:" lines and do not change the verdict or the exit
status; for example win32\-input\-mode being on, which Windows Terminal and ConPTY normally have: it matters only
if the key records arrive damaged (test that with \fB\-\-tvision\fP). The tty's device name is printed too, and, when \fBWINCYGTERM\fP is set in the environment, a \fBvia:\fP
line with the version of
.BR wincygterm (1)
that started the session. When
a dropped OSC 11 happens on a Cygwin console (\fI/dev/cons*\fP) the verdict says what to do:
run the program under
.BR wincygterm (1),
in mintty, or over ssh.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-timeout " SECONDS"
How long to wait for the replies (default 2.0).
.TP
.BI \-\-label " TEXT"
A name for this run, shown in the output, to tell runs apart.
.TP
.B \-\-raw
Also show every byte that came back.
.TP
.B \-\-input
Also test keyboard and mouse fidelity, interactively: SGR mouse reporting is turned on and you are asked to type
\fIabcdefghij\fP, click three times and press Up, Down, Left, Right. Dropped keystrokes, missing clicks,
stray bytes and win32\-input\-mode records are reported.
.TP
.B \-\-tvision
Like \fB\-\-input\fP, but the terminal is first set up as tvision sets it up at startup (win32\-input\-mode,
kitty keys, modifyOtherKeys, mouse) and the win32\-input\-mode key records are decoded. Records that were cut in
two across reads are counted, together with the time the rest took.
.TP
.B \-\-dos
Also test the classic DOS / Windows screen\-control set. Cursor behaviour is measured, not eyeballed: each
ANSI.SYS sequence (CUP, HVP, CUU, CUD, CUF, CUB, save/restore, plus the screen edges, \fBESC[2J\fP and line wrap,
including ANSI.SYS's \fBESC[=7h\fP) is followed by \fBESC[6n\fP and the reported position is compared with what DOS
would do. Colours and attributes (bold, underline, blink, reverse, conceal, the 16 foreground and background
colours, bright backgrounds, 256 colours, true colour) are read back with DECRQSS, the DOS cursor shape and
hide/show with DECSCUSR and DECRQM, and each of the 256 CP437 glyphs is checked to take exactly one column. The
tests run on the alternate screen where there is one. Afterwards a picture is drawn (the 256 DOS attribute bytes,
the CP437 table, erase\-to\-end\-of\-line, DEC line drawing), each line saying what you should see.
.IP
The mouse is tested both ways, interactively (click three times, twice, 8 seconds each): as escape\-sequence
reports (xterm SGR, as tvision, ncurses and vim use it) and as console events (\fBENABLE_MOUSE_INPUT\fP and
\fBMOUSE_EVENT\fP records, as Microsoft Edit and other Windows\-API programs read it). The second needs a Windows
console program: on native Windows it runs in the process; under Cygwin or MSYS a native Windows Python (\fBpy\fP or
\fBpython.exe\fP on the PATH) is started from the shell, which is exactly a Windows program started from there. A
pty that carries only text, as under
.BR wincygterm (1)
without \fBwcdirect\fP, shows as: escape\-sequence mouse works, console events do not.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-visual
With \fB\-\-dos\fP: skip the picture.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-mouse
With \fB\-\-dos\fP: skip the interactive mouse tests.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-vt\-input
Native Windows only: leave ENABLE_VIRTUAL_TERMINAL_INPUT off, as for a program that reads key events.

.SH SUMMARY
Every run, whatever the options, ends with a \fBSUMMARY\fP of the findings, one per line, tagged \fB[ ok ]\fP,
\fB[warn]\fP (a capability is missing), \fB[FAIL]\fP (a problem with the path: dropped colour query, mangled
replies, failed input test) or \fB[info]\fP, and a count of failures and warnings. It covers the verdicts above,
who the terminal says it is, the DECRQM modes, and, with \fB\-\-dos\fP, \fB\-\-input\fP or \fB\-\-tvision\fP, their
results. \fB\-\-dos\fP never changes the exit status: a terminal without blink is a fact, not a fault.

.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Everything clean.
.TP
.B 1
A problem was found (OSC 11 dropped or silent, negotiation mangled, or the input test failed).
.TP
.B 2
There is no terminal to ask.

.SH EXAMPLES
Compare a few ways of reaching the same machine:
.PP
.nf
termprobe \-\-label mintty
ssh \-tt host termprobe \-\-label ssh
termprobe \-\-tvision \-\-label tvmail\-repro
.fi

.SH NOTES
A program that waits only a few milliseconds for the rest of a key sequence takes a lone ESC for the Escape key.
Over some paths (Windows Terminal with win32\-input\-mode, through ssh and a Cygwin pty), key records arrive cut
in two with the rest 16 to 33 ms behind; ncurses by default waits 10 ms (ESCDELAY and its read timeout), so the
remainder is typed as text, for example
.BR [68;32;100;1;0;1_ .
\fB\-\-tvision\fP reproduces exactly that and measures the gap.

A program can turn win32\-input\-mode on itself, so sending ESC [ ? 9001 l beforehand does not help for such a
program.

Cygwin's own console (\fI/dev/cons*\fP) swallows OSC strings on their way out, so an OSC query never reaches
the terminal there; mintty and ssh sessions, which have a pty, are other paths.

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR wincygterm (1),
.BR tcping (1)
